Implemented Vercel Web Analytics for the Schwingen ML project.

## Changes Made

### 1. Installed @vercel/analytics Package
- Added `@vercel/analytics` version 2.0.1 to project dependencies
- Used npm to install the package (project's package manager)
- Updated package-lock.json accordingly

### 2. Modified Files

**app/layout.tsx**
- Added import: `import { Analytics } from "@vercel/analytics/next";`
- Added `<Analytics />` component at the end of the body element (after footer)
- Followed Next.js App Router best practices from official Vercel documentation

**package.json**
- Added `"@vercel/analytics": "^2.0.1"` to dependencies

**package-lock.json**
- Updated with all dependencies for @vercel/analytics package

## Implementation Details

The implementation follows the official Vercel Web Analytics quickstart guide fetched from https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart on October 6, 2026.

For Next.js App Router projects:
- The Analytics component is imported from `@vercel/analytics/next`
- It's placed at the end of the body in the root layout file
- This enables automatic page view tracking for all routes

## Verification

✅ Build completed successfully with `npm run build`
✅ TypeScript compilation passed without errors
✅ All routes generated correctly (16 pages)
✅ No breaking changes to existing functionality

## Next Steps

To enable analytics tracking:
1. Deploy this project to Vercel
2. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard under the Analytics section
3. Analytics will automatically start tracking page views once deployed

The integration is complete and ready for deployment. The Analytics component will only send data when deployed on Vercel, so there's no impact on local development.
